title: Python Application Spawning a Shell Reverse Connection (Cairn / MONAI Label Intrusion)
id: d9fa78f0-21b5-4b53-9082-a0fabd945518
status: experimental
description: >
    Detects a Python web application process spawning a Unix shell that
    carries reverse-connection syntax. Amazon Threat Intelligence described
    exactly this final step against an internet-facing MONAI Label server:
    autonomous Cairn agents used a path traversal in the datastore upload
    endpoint (CVE-2026-133968) to write a Python reverse shell into a file the
    interpreter reads at startup, then obtained a root shell. The parent side
    is the Python interpreter hosting the exposed application; the child side
    is a shell whose command line redirects a socket back to the operator.
    Any hit on a server that has no business running shell one-liners is worth
    immediate triage.
references:
    - https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/chinese-nexus-actors-cairn-ai-agents-found-a-zero-day-on-their-own/
    - https://thehackernews.com/2026/11/attackers-ai-agents-found-zero-day-in.html
    - https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/hackers-use-autonomous-ai-tool-cairn-to-find-zero-day-take-over-server/
author: Tia N. List
date: 2026/11/22
tags:
    - attack.t1190
    - attack.t1059
    - attack.t1546
logsource:
    category: process_creation
    product: linux
detection:
    selection_parent:
        ParentImage|endswith:
            - '/python3'
            - '/python'
            - '/python3.11'
            - '/python3.12'
            - '/python3.13'
    selection_shell:
        Image|endswith:
            - '/sh'
            - '/bash'
            - '/dash'
            - '/ash'
            - '/zsh'
    selection_reverse:
        CommandLine|contains:
            - '/dev/tcp/'
            - '/dev/udp/'
            - 'bash -i'
            - 'nc -e'
            - 'ncat -e'
            - 'mkfifo'
            - '0>&1'
            - 'socket.socket'
    condition: selection_parent and selection_shell and selection_reverse
falsepositives:
    - Developer workstations and CI jobs where a Python script legitimately launches a shell one-liner that mentions a socket or /dev/tcp redirect
    - Health-check or diagnostic scripts maintained by an application team
level: high
